% Metainfo: Parsing .torrent Files
% Chapter 2 of the literate BitTorrent client

\section{Metainfo Files}

A \texttt{.torrent} file, also called a \emph{metainfo} file, contains all the
information needed to download a torrent: where to find peers (the tracker URL),
how to verify downloaded data (piece hashes), and what files comprise the torrent.
The entire file is bencoded, making our decoder from the previous chapter the
foundation for this one.

\subsection{The Metainfo Structure}

At the top level, a metainfo file is a bencoded dictionary with two required keys:

\begin{description}
\item[announce] A string containing the tracker URL. Peers register with this
  URL to discover each other.
\item[info] A dictionary containing metadata about the torrent's content,
  including file names, sizes, piece length, and piece hashes.
\end{description}

The \texttt{info} dictionary is special: its bencoded form is hashed with SHA1
to produce the \emph{info hash}, a 20-byte identifier that uniquely identifies
this torrent across the entire BitTorrent network. Every peer sharing this
content uses the same info hash.

\subsection{Single-File vs Multi-File Torrents}

The info dictionary supports two formats:

\begin{description}
\item[Single-file] Contains a \texttt{name} (filename) and \texttt{length}
  (file size in bytes). The torrent represents exactly one file.

\item[Multi-file] Contains a \texttt{name} (directory name) and \texttt{files}
  (a list of file entries). Each file entry has a \texttt{length} and a
  \texttt{path} (list of path components from the root directory).
\end{description}

We unify these into a single representation: a torrent is always a list of
files, with single-file torrents being the degenerate case of a one-element list.

\subsection{The FileInfo Structure}

Each file in a torrent is represented by its length and path. For single-file
torrents, the path is simply the filename. For multi-file torrents, the path
includes directory components.

<<file info struct>>=
/// Information about a single file within a torrent.
///
/// For single-file torrents, this represents the entire torrent content.
/// For multi-file torrents, each file in the torrent has its own `FileInfo`.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct FileInfo {
    /// Size of the file in bytes.
    pub length: u64,

    /// Path to the file, relative to the torrent's root directory.
    ///
    /// For single-file torrents, this is just the filename.
    /// For multi-file torrents, this includes directory components.
    pub path: PathBuf,
}
@

The path is stored as a [[PathBuf]] rather than a [[String]] because file paths
can contain arbitrary bytes in BitTorrent (the protocol uses byte strings, not
UTF-8 strings). [[PathBuf]] handles this correctly on most platforms.

\subsection{The Metainfo Structure}

The main [[Metainfo]] struct captures everything we need from a torrent file:

<<metainfo struct>>=
/// Parsed contents of a .torrent file.
///
/// This structure contains all the information needed to download a torrent:
/// - Where to find peers (tracker URL)
/// - How to identify the torrent (info hash)
/// - How to verify downloaded data (piece hashes)
/// - What files comprise the torrent (file list)
///
/// The structure unifies single-file and multi-file torrents into a consistent
/// representation where every torrent has a list of files (single-file torrents
/// simply have a one-element list).
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Metainfo {
    /// The tracker's announce URL.
    ///
    /// Peers register with this URL to discover other peers sharing the torrent.
    /// The URL typically uses HTTP or UDP protocol.
    pub announce: String,

    /// The 20-byte SHA1 hash of the bencoded info dictionary.
    ///
    /// This uniquely identifies the torrent across the BitTorrent network.
    /// All peers sharing the same content have the same info hash.
    pub info_hash: [u8; 20],

    /// Size of each piece in bytes.
    ///
    /// Pieces are the unit of data transfer and verification in BitTorrent.
    /// Typical values are powers of two between 256KB (262144) and 4MB (4194304).
    /// The last piece may be smaller than this value.
    pub piece_length: u32,

    /// SHA1 hashes of each piece, in order.
    ///
    /// After downloading a piece, its SHA1 hash is computed and compared to
    /// the corresponding entry here. A mismatch indicates corruption.
    pub piece_hashes: Vec<[u8; 20]>,

    /// Total size of the torrent content in bytes.
    ///
    /// This is the sum of all file lengths. For single-file torrents, this
    /// equals the file's length. For multi-file torrents, it's the total
    /// across all files.
    pub total_length: u64,

    /// List of files in the torrent.
    ///
    /// Single-file torrents have exactly one entry. Multi-file torrents have
    /// one entry per file. Files are stored in the order they appear in the
    /// metainfo, which determines how piece boundaries align with file content.
    pub files: Vec<FileInfo>,

    /// Name of the torrent.
    ///
    /// For single-file torrents, this is the filename.
    /// For multi-file torrents, this is the root directory name.
    pub name: String,
}
@

Several design decisions deserve explanation:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{info\_hash as fixed array}: We use \texttt{[u8; 20]} rather than
  \texttt{Vec<u8>} because SHA1 hashes are always exactly 20 bytes. The fixed
  size prevents accidental misuse and enables efficient comparison.

\item \textbf{piece\_length as u32}: Piece lengths are typically between 256KB
  and 4MB, well within u32 range. Using u32 saves memory and matches the
  protocol's 32-bit piece index.

\item \textbf{piece\_hashes as Vec}: The number of pieces varies widely---a small
  torrent might have 10 pieces, while a large one could have 100,000. A vector
  provides the flexibility needed.

\item \textbf{total\_length as u64}: Modern torrents can exceed 4GB, requiring
  64-bit file sizes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Error Handling}

Parsing metainfo can fail in several ways. We define a dedicated error type
to provide clear feedback about what went wrong.

<<metainfo errors>>=
/// Errors that can occur when parsing a metainfo file.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum MetainfoError {
    /// A required field is missing from the metainfo dictionary.
    #[error("missing required field: {0}")]
    MissingField(&'static str),

    /// The piece length is invalid (zero, negative, or too large).
    #[error("invalid piece length: {0}")]
    InvalidPieceLength(String),

    /// The pieces field is malformed (not a multiple of 20 bytes).
    #[error("invalid piece hashes: {0}")]
    InvalidPieceHashes(String),

    /// The multi-file torrent has an invalid files array.
    #[error("invalid multi-file torrent: {0}")]
    InvalidMultiFile(String),
}
@

These error variants cover the main parsing failure modes:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{MissingField}: A required key (like ``announce'' or ``info'')
  is absent from the dictionary. The parameter identifies which field.

\item \textbf{InvalidPieceLength}: The piece length is zero (would cause
  division by zero), negative, or unreasonably large.

\item \textbf{InvalidPieceHashes}: The ``pieces'' field isn't a valid
  concatenation of 20-byte SHA1 hashes. This happens when its length
  isn't a multiple of 20.

\item \textbf{InvalidMultiFile}: The multi-file torrent's ``files'' array
  is malformed. This can happen when entries lack required fields, have
  invalid types, or contain empty paths.
\end{itemize}

