\subsection{Helper Functions for BencodeValue Extraction}

Parsing metainfo requires extracting specific types from [[BencodeValue]]. Rather than
repeating match expressions throughout the parsing code, we define helper functions
that perform type-checked extraction with clear error messages.

These helpers follow a common pattern: given a dictionary and a key, extract the
value and verify it has the expected type. If the key is missing or the value has
the wrong type, return an appropriate error.

<<helper functions>>=
/// Extracts a dictionary from a BencodeValue, returning an error if not a Dict.
fn get_dict(value: &BencodeValue) -> Result<&BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>, MetainfoError> {
    match value {
        BencodeValue::Dict(d) => Ok(d),
        _ => Err(MetainfoError::MissingField("expected dictionary")),
    }
}

/// Extracts a byte string from a dictionary by key.
fn get_bytes<'a>(
    dict: &'a BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>,
    key: &'static str,
) -> Result<&'a [u8], MetainfoError> {
    match dict.get(key.as_bytes()) {
        Some(BencodeValue::Bytes(b)) => Ok(b),
        Some(_) => Err(MetainfoError::MissingField(key)), // wrong type
        None => Err(MetainfoError::MissingField(key)),
    }
}

/// Extracts a UTF-8 string from a dictionary by key.
fn get_string(
    dict: &BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>,
    key: &'static str,
) -> Result<String, MetainfoError> {
    let bytes = get_bytes(dict, key)?;
    String::from_utf8(bytes.to_vec()).map_err(|_| MetainfoError::MissingField(key))
}

/// Extracts an integer from a dictionary by key.
fn get_int(
    dict: &BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>,
    key: &'static str,
) -> Result<i64, MetainfoError> {
    match dict.get(key.as_bytes()) {
        Some(BencodeValue::Integer(n)) => Ok(*n),
        Some(_) => Err(MetainfoError::MissingField(key)), // wrong type
        None => Err(MetainfoError::MissingField(key)),
    }
}

/// Extracts a nested dictionary from a dictionary by key.
fn get_dict_field<'a>(
    dict: &'a BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>,
    key: &'static str,
) -> Result<&'a BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>, MetainfoError> {
    match dict.get(key.as_bytes()) {
        Some(BencodeValue::Dict(d)) => Ok(d),
        Some(_) => Err(MetainfoError::MissingField(key)), // wrong type
        None => Err(MetainfoError::MissingField(key)),
    }
}

/// Extracts a BencodeValue from a dictionary by key.
///
/// Unlike `get_dict_field` which extracts only the inner BTreeMap,
/// this returns the BencodeValue itself, which is needed when we
/// need to re-encode the value (e.g., for info hash computation).
fn get_value<'a>(
    dict: &'a BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>,
    key: &'static str,
) -> Result<&'a BencodeValue, MetainfoError> {
    dict.get(key.as_bytes())
        .ok_or(MetainfoError::MissingField(key))
}

/// Extracts a list from a dictionary by key.
fn get_list<'a>(
    dict: &'a BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>,
    key: &'static str,
) -> Result<&'a Vec<BencodeValue>, MetainfoError> {
    match dict.get(key.as_bytes()) {
        Some(BencodeValue::List(l)) => Ok(l),
        Some(_) => Err(MetainfoError::MissingField(key)), // wrong type
        None => Err(MetainfoError::MissingField(key)),
    }
}
@

Each function returns a [[MetainfoError]] rather than a generic error because these
are specifically designed for metainfo parsing. The error messages are intentionally
simple---in production code, you might want more detailed type mismatch messages.

\subsection{Computing the Info Hash}

The \emph{info hash} is a 20-byte SHA1 hash of the bencoded info dictionary. This
hash uniquely identifies a torrent across the entire BitTorrent network---every
peer sharing the same content uses the same info hash to find each other.

Computing the info hash requires:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Extracting the info dictionary as a [[BencodeValue]]
\item Re-encoding it to bytes using our bencode encoder
\item Computing the SHA1 hash of those bytes
\end{enumerate}

The re-encoding step is critical because we need the \emph{canonical} byte
representation. Our [[BTreeMap]]-based [[BencodeValue::Dict]] ensures keys are
sorted, which is required by the bencode specification. This guarantees that
any encoder will produce the same bytes for the same dictionary content.

<<info hash computation>>=
/// Computes the SHA1 info hash from a BencodeValue representing the info dict.
///
/// The info hash is computed by:
/// 1. Encoding the info dictionary to bencode bytes
/// 2. Computing the SHA1 hash of those bytes
///
/// This is the canonical way to compute info hashes: by re-encoding the
/// parsed info dictionary rather than extracting raw bytes from the original
/// torrent file. Our BTreeMap-based encoding ensures proper key ordering.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `info_dict` - The info dictionary as a BencodeValue (must be a Dict)
///
/// # Returns
///
/// A 20-byte array containing the SHA1 hash.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let info_value = get_value(root, "info")?;
/// let info_hash = compute_info_hash(info_value);
/// ```
pub fn compute_info_hash(info_dict: &BencodeValue) -> [u8; 20] {
    // Encode the info dict to canonical bencode bytes
    let encoded = encode(info_dict);

    // Compute SHA1 hash
    let mut hasher = Sha1::new();
    hasher.update(&encoded);
    let result = hasher.finalize();

    // Convert GenericArray to fixed-size array
    let mut hash = [0u8; 20];
    hash.copy_from_slice(&result);
    hash
}
@

Note that we encode from the [[BencodeValue]] rather than using the original
bytes from the torrent file. While using original bytes might seem more direct,
it would require tracking byte positions during parsing. Re-encoding is simpler
and equally correct because our encoder produces canonical output.

\subsection{Parsing Piece Hashes}

The ``pieces'' field in a torrent contains the concatenation of all piece SHA1 hashes.
Each SHA1 hash is exactly 20 bytes, so the total length must be a multiple of 20.
We validate this constraint and split the bytes into a vector of fixed-size arrays.

<<parse pieces>>=
/// Parses the pieces byte string into a vector of 20-byte SHA1 hashes.
///
/// The pieces field is a concatenation of SHA1 hashes, one per piece.
/// This function validates that the length is a multiple of 20 and splits
/// the bytes into individual hash arrays.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns `InvalidPieceHashes` if the length is not a multiple of 20.
fn parse_pieces(pieces_bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<[u8; 20]>, MetainfoError> {
    if pieces_bytes.len() % 20 != 0 {
        return Err(MetainfoError::InvalidPieceHashes(format!(
            "pieces length {} is not a multiple of 20",
            pieces_bytes.len()
        )));
    }

    let num_pieces = pieces_bytes.len() / 20;
    let mut hashes = Vec::with_capacity(num_pieces);

    for chunk in pieces_bytes.chunks_exact(20) {
        let mut hash = [0u8; 20];
        hash.copy_from_slice(chunk);
        hashes.push(hash);
    }

    Ok(hashes)
}
@

Using [[chunks\_exact]] ensures we get exactly 20-byte slices with no remainder.
The capacity pre-allocation avoids reallocations as we build the vector.

\subsection{Parsing Single-File Torrents}

A single-file torrent has a simple info dictionary: just a ``name'' and ``length''.
The name becomes both the torrent name and the filename, and the length is the
total content size.

<<parse single file>>=
/// Parses file information for a single-file torrent.
///
/// Single-file torrents have `length` directly in the info dict rather than
/// a `files` array. The `name` field is used as the filename.
///
/// Returns a tuple of (FileInfo, total_length) where total_length equals
/// the single file's length.
fn parse_single_file(
    info_dict: &BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>,
    name: &str,
) -> Result<(Vec<FileInfo>, u64), MetainfoError> {
    let length = get_int(info_dict, "length")?;

    if length < 0 {
        return Err(MetainfoError::MissingField("length"));
    }

    let length = length as u64;
    let file_info = FileInfo {
        length,
        path: PathBuf::from(name),
    };

    Ok((vec![file_info], length))
}
@

The function returns both the file list (a single-element vector) and the total
length. For single-file torrents, these are effectively the same, but returning
both maintains consistency with how we handle multi-file torrents.

\subsection{Parsing Multi-File Torrents}

Multi-file torrents store file information differently from single-file torrents.
Instead of a single [[length]] field, they have a [[files]] array. Each entry in
this array is a dictionary with [[length]] (file size in bytes) and [[path]]
(a list of path components).

The [[name]] field in multi-file torrents specifies the root directory name.
All file paths are relative to this directory. For example, a torrent with
name ``mydir'' and a file with path [[["subdir", "file.txt"]]] would result
in the path ``mydir/subdir/file.txt''.

<<parse multi file>>=
/// Parses file information for a multi-file torrent.
///
/// Multi-file torrents have a `files` array in the info dict. Each entry
/// contains:
/// - `length`: size of the file in bytes
/// - `path`: list of path components (byte strings)
///
/// The torrent's `name` field becomes the root directory for all paths.
///
/// Returns a tuple of (files, total_length) where total_length is the
/// sum of all individual file lengths.
fn parse_multi_file(
    info_dict: &BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, BencodeValue>,
    name: &str,
) -> Result<(Vec<FileInfo>, u64), MetainfoError> {
    let files_list = get_list(info_dict, "files")?;

    if files_list.is_empty() {
        return Err(MetainfoError::InvalidMultiFile(
            "files array is empty".to_string(),
        ));
    }

    let mut files = Vec::with_capacity(files_list.len());
    let mut total_length: u64 = 0;

    for (index, file_entry) in files_list.iter().enumerate() {
        // Each file entry must be a dictionary
        let file_dict = match file_entry {
            BencodeValue::Dict(d) => d,
            _ => {
                return Err(MetainfoError::InvalidMultiFile(format!(
                    "file entry {} is not a dictionary",
                    index
                )));
            }
        };

        // Extract file length
        let length = get_int(file_dict, "length").map_err(|_| {
            MetainfoError::InvalidMultiFile(format!(
                "file entry {} missing or invalid length",
                index
            ))
        })?;

        if length < 0 {
            return Err(MetainfoError::InvalidMultiFile(format!(
                "file entry {} has negative length",
                index
            )));
        }
        let length = length as u64;

        // Extract path components
        let path_list = get_list(file_dict, "path").map_err(|_| {
            MetainfoError::InvalidMultiFile(format!(
                "file entry {} missing or invalid path",
                index
            ))
        })?;

        if path_list.is_empty() {
            return Err(MetainfoError::InvalidMultiFile(format!(
                "file entry {} has empty path",
                index
            )));
        }

        // Build the path from components
        // Start with the torrent name as root directory
        let mut path = PathBuf::from(name);

        for (comp_index, component) in path_list.iter().enumerate() {
            match component {
                BencodeValue::Bytes(bytes) => {
                    // Convert bytes to string for the path component
                    // Use lossy conversion to handle non-UTF8 bytes
                    let component_str = String::from_utf8_lossy(bytes);
                    if component_str.is_empty() {
                        return Err(MetainfoError::InvalidMultiFile(format!(
                            "file entry {} has empty path component at index {}",
                            index, comp_index
                        )));
                    }
                    path.push(component_str.as_ref());
                }
                _ => {
                    return Err(MetainfoError::InvalidMultiFile(format!(
                        "file entry {} path component {} is not a byte string",
                        index, comp_index
                    )));
                }
            }
        }

        files.push(FileInfo { length, path });

        // Accumulate total length, checking for overflow
        total_length = total_length.checked_add(length).ok_or_else(|| {
            MetainfoError::InvalidMultiFile("total length overflow".to_string())
        })?;
    }

    Ok((files, total_length))
}
@

The path building deserves careful attention. We start with the torrent's name
as the root directory, then append each path component. This matches the
BitTorrent specification where multi-file torrents create a directory structure.

For path components, we use [[String::from\_utf8\_lossy]] to handle the common
case where paths are valid UTF-8 (most filenames), while still accepting
arbitrary byte sequences (the protocol allows any bytes). Invalid UTF-8
sequences are replaced with the Unicode replacement character.

We also validate that neither the path array nor individual components are
empty, and check for potential overflow when accumulating the total length.
These edge cases may seem unlikely, but malformed torrents exist in the wild.

\subsection{The Main Parse Function}

The [[parse]] function is the main entry point for parsing metainfo files. It
coordinates all the pieces: decoding the bencode, extracting fields, and
constructing the [[Metainfo]] struct.

<<parse function>>=
/// Parses raw bytes into a Metainfo structure.
///
/// This is the main entry point for parsing .torrent files. The input should
/// be the complete contents of a .torrent file (bencoded dictionary).
///
/// Supports both single-file torrents (with `length` field) and multi-file
/// torrents (with `files` array). Multi-file torrents have the torrent name
/// as the root directory, with file paths relative to that directory.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns an error if:
/// - The input is not valid bencode
/// - Required fields are missing (announce, info, name, piece length, pieces)
/// - The pieces length is not a multiple of 20
/// - The piece length is invalid (zero or negative)
/// - Neither `length` nor `files` field is present
/// - Multi-file torrent has malformed files array
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let torrent_bytes = std::fs::read("example.torrent")?;
/// let metainfo = parse(&torrent_bytes)?;
/// println!("Tracker: {}", metainfo.announce);
/// ```
pub fn parse(data: &[u8]) -> Result<Metainfo, MetainfoError> {
    // Decode the bencoded data
    let value = decode(data).map_err(|e| match e {
        BencodeError::UnexpectedEof => MetainfoError::MissingField("valid bencode"),
        _ => MetainfoError::MissingField("valid bencode"),
    })?;

    // The top level must be a dictionary
    let root = get_dict(&value)?;

    // Extract announce URL
    let announce = get_string(root, "announce")?;

    // Extract the info dictionary as BencodeValue (for info hash computation)
    let info_value = get_value(root, "info")?;

    // Also extract as BTreeMap for field access
    let info_dict = get_dict_field(root, "info")?;

    // Extract name from info dict
    let name = get_string(info_dict, "name")?;

    // Extract piece length
    let piece_length_i64 = get_int(info_dict, "piece length")?;
    if piece_length_i64 <= 0 {
        return Err(MetainfoError::InvalidPieceLength(format!(
            "piece length must be positive, got {}",
            piece_length_i64
        )));
    }
    if piece_length_i64 > u32::MAX as i64 {
        return Err(MetainfoError::InvalidPieceLength(format!(
            "piece length {} exceeds maximum",
            piece_length_i64
        )));
    }
    let piece_length = piece_length_i64 as u32;

    // Extract and parse pieces
    let pieces_bytes = get_bytes(info_dict, "pieces")?;
    let piece_hashes = parse_pieces(pieces_bytes)?;

    // Determine if this is single-file or multi-file format
    // Single-file has "length" in info dict; multi-file has "files" array
    let (files, total_length) = if info_dict.contains_key(b"length".as_ref()) {
        parse_single_file(info_dict, &name)?
    } else if info_dict.contains_key(b"files".as_ref()) {
        parse_multi_file(info_dict, &name)?
    } else {
        // Neither single-file nor multi-file format
        return Err(MetainfoError::MissingField("length or files"));
    };

    // Compute the info hash from the info dictionary
    let info_hash = compute_info_hash(info_value);

    Ok(Metainfo {
        announce,
        info_hash,
        piece_length,
        piece_hashes,
        total_length,
        files,
        name,
    })
}
@

The parse function is careful about error handling. Each extraction step can fail,
and we propagate errors with descriptive messages. The bencode decoding error is
mapped to a [[MissingField]] error since the specific bencode error details are
less relevant at this level.

The info hash is computed by extracting the info dictionary as a [[BencodeValue]],
re-encoding it to bytes, and computing the SHA1 hash. This canonical approach
ensures consistent hashes regardless of how the original torrent file was structured.

\subsection{Tests}

We test the parsing with hand-crafted minimal torrent data. This gives us precise
control over the test input and lets us verify each edge case.

<<metainfo tests>>=
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    /// Helper to create a minimal valid single-file torrent in bencode format.
    ///
    /// The structure is:
    /// ```text
    /// d
    ///   8:announce <url>
    ///   4:info d
    ///     6:length <bytes>
    ///     4:name <filename>
    ///     12:piece length <bytes per piece>
    ///     6:pieces <20 * N bytes of hashes>
    ///   e
    /// e
    /// ```
    fn make_single_file_torrent(
        announce: &str,
        name: &str,
        length: i64,
        piece_length: i64,
        pieces: &[u8],
    ) -> Vec<u8> {
        use crate::bencode::encode;

        let mut info = BTreeMap::new();
        info.insert(b"length".to_vec(), BencodeValue::Integer(length));
        info.insert(b"name".to_vec(), BencodeValue::Bytes(name.as_bytes().to_vec()));
        info.insert(
            b"piece length".to_vec(),
            BencodeValue::Integer(piece_length),
        );
        info.insert(b"pieces".to_vec(), BencodeValue::Bytes(pieces.to_vec()));

        let mut root = BTreeMap::new();
